cost`)."},"1855":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-609","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-direct-non-iterative","content":"All ops use **weight 1.0** with all shape constants in `flop_cost`.  Per-matrix\ncost is multiplied by the batch dimension product for stacked inputs.  Zero-dim\nmatrices charge 0."},"1856":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-610","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-direct-non-iterative","content":"**Complex dtypes**: most of the dense linalg family bills `complex_factor = 4` — a\ncomplex factorization does roughly `4×` the real arithmetic of its real counterpart —\nand `linalg.matrix_power` and `linalg.multi_dot` bill this flat `4` as well. The\nexceptions are the ops that mirror a bare `fnp.*` twin and take *its* complex factor\ninstead of this dense rule: `linalg.outer` bills factor 6 like `outer`; `linalg.matmul`,\n`linalg.tensordot`, and `linalg.vecdot` bill the same **exact** per-call contraction\nfactor as `matmul`/`tensordot`/`vecdot`; `linalg.trace` bills 2 like `trace` (a diagonal\nsum, not a factorization); and `linalg.cross` bills 4.7 like `cross`. (Where such a row\nstill carries `complex_factor = 4.0` in the registry it is inert metadata — the\ndelegation or the op-specific factor applies instead.)"},"1857":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-611","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-direct-non-iterative","content":"Op"},"1858":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-612","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-direct-non-iterative","content":"flop\\_cost (per matrix)"},"1859":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-6