","content":"`blacklisted`"},"2734":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-75","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Intentionally unsupported"},"2735":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-76","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"Raises `AttributeError`"},"2736":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-77","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"**Free operations** include allocation (`zeros`, `ones`, `empty`), shape manipulation (`reshape`, `transpose`, `squeeze`), indexing helpers (`indices`), and metadata queries (`shape`, `ndim`, `size`). These do not touch the budget."},"2737":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-78","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"**Blocked operations** include I/O (`save`, `load`), error state management (`geterr`, `seterr`), and other operations that do not make sense in a FLOP-counted context. Calling a blocked operation raises `AttributeError`."},"2738":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-79","page_id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works#the-operation-registry","content":"When per-operation weights are loaded, the analytical cost is multiplied by the operation's weight before deduction. This allows the cost model to reflect that `exp` is more expensive than `abs` in terms of actual hardware instructions, while keeping the base formulas simple and deterministic."},"2739":{"id":"/docs/understanding/how-flopscope-works-80","page_id":"/docs/underst