,"content":"`fnp.random.randn`, `fnp.random.normal`, `fnp.random.uniform`"},"2794":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-51","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#counted-operations-cost--0","content":"Stats"},"2795":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-52","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#counted-operations-cost--0","content":"flat per-element (varies)"},"2796":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-53","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#counted-operations-cost--0","content":"`flops.stats.norm.pdf`, `flops.stats.expon.cdf`, `flops.stats.cauchy.ppf`"},"2797":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-54","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#counted-operations-cost--0","content":"When inputs are `SymmetricTensor`, many operations automatically get reduced costs. See Exploit Symmetry."},"2798":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-55","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#reduction-cost-surfaces","content":"flopscope splits reductions into two cost surfaces:"},"2799":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-56","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#reduction-cost-surfaces","content":"**Tier 1 — additive accumulation** (`sum`, `prod`, `max`, `min`, `all`, `any`, bitwise/logical `or`/`and`/`xor`, `mean`).\nCharges `(unique input entries − unique output cells) + extra ops`. `mean` adds one divide per unique output cell."},"2800":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-57","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#reduction-cost-surfaces","content":"**Tier 2 — selection-style reductions** (`median`, `percentile`, `quantile`).\nCharges `(unique output cells) × (per-output cost)`. Each output cell needs a full partition pass over the reduced axes; symmetry helps when output cells share an orbit."},"2801":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-58","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#reduction-cost-surfaces","content":"See Symmetry-aware FLOP counting for the mental model and worked examples."},"2802":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-59","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#blacklisted-operations","content":"Operations not relevant to numerical computation. Calling them raises an `AttributeError`. These are I/O, configuration, datetime, and display functions that have no meaningful FLOP cost."},"2803":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-60","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#blacklisted-operations","content":"**Blacklisted categories:** I/O (`save`, `load`, `loadtxt`, `savetxt`, `savez`, `genfromtxt`), configuration (`seterr`, `geterr`, `setbufsize`), datetime (`busday_count`, `is_busday`), display (`array2string`, `array_repr`), functional (`apply_along_axis`, `piecewise`, `frompyfunc`)."},"2804":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-61","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#blacklisted-operations","content":"See API Reference for the complete list."},"2805":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-62","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#related-pages","content":"API Reference — complete list of every operation and its category"},"2806":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-63","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#related-pages","content":"FLOP Counting Model — how costs are calculated"},"2807":{"id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories-64","page_id":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/operation-categories#related-pages","content":"Migrate from NumPy — what changes when moving from NumPy"},"2808":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"page","content":"Symmetry-aware FLOP counting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Understanding Flopscope"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection"},"2809":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-0","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#the-mental-model","content":"The mental model"},"2810":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-1","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"Einsum: one event per orbit of (multiplications, accumulations)"},"2811":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-2","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"Reductions: `(unique inputs − unique outputs) + extra ops`"},"2812":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-3","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Inspecting what gets charged"},"2813":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-4","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"When numbers might surprise you"},"2814":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-5","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#whats-actually-happening","content":"What's actually happening"},"2815":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-6","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"*How flopscope reduces the FLOPs charged for `einsum`, `sum`, `mean`, `median`, and friends when your tensors have symmetric structure.*"},"2816":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-7","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"**You will learn:**"},"2817":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-8","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"The one mental model flopscope uses for all symmetry-aware FLOP savings"},"2818":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-9","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How that model applies to `einsum`"},"2819":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-10","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How it applies to reductions (`sum`, `mean`, `median`, …)"},"2820":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-11","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"How to inspect what gets charged with `flops.einsum_accumulation_cost` and `flops.reduction_accumulation_cost`"},"2821":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-12","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","content":"When the savings don't materialize, and why"},"2822":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-13","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#the-mental-model","content":"When you declare symmetry on a tensor (with `flops.as_symmetric`) — or when flopscope detects it from a repeated operand object — some entries in the dense layout are mathematically interchangeable. An **orbit** groups together all entries that are interchangeable. flopscope charges one event per orbit, not per dense element."},"2823":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-14","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#the-mental-model","content":"For an `(n, n)` matrix declared symmetric in axes `(0, 1)`, there are `n*(n+1)/2` orbits among the `n*n` entries (the off-diagonal pairs share an orbit; diagonal entries are singleton orbits). Any cost that scales with \"number of distinct entries\" drops accordingly."},"2824":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-15","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"For an einsum, flopscope's charge in plain terms is:"},"2825":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-16","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"where `k` is the operand count. The savings come from *output orbits* — distinct output cells that flopscope can prove are interchangeable. The output gets symmetric structure either from declared symmetry on operands or from a repeated operand object appearing in multiple positions."},"2826":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-17","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"Worked example — triple outer product `'i,j,k->ijk'` of three rank-1 vectors of length 4:"},"2827":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-18","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"When you pass three distinct vectors, every (i, j, k) output cell is its own orbit — 192 events. When you pass the *same* vector in all three operand positions, the operand swap induces an `S₃` action on the output labels, collapsing the 64 dense output cells into 20 orbits — 60 events. The output is structurally symmetric and the cost reflects that."},"2828":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-19","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#einsum-one-event-per-orbit-of-multiplications-accumulations","content":"The path that opt\\_einsum picks is irrelevant — the charge is **path-independent**. For multi-component expressions, each connected component is handled independently. Participants don't need to know which internal classifier fires for any given expression; only that the result is exact."},"2829":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-20","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"For reductions (`sum`, `prod`, `max`, `min`, `mean`, `median`, …), the formula is:"},"2830":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-21","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"The `− unique output cells` term is the off-by-one fix from #56: the first input element of each output cell is a free copy; only the remaining accumulations cost. So `sum` on `(10,)` charges 9 flops, not 10:"},"2831":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-22","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"With declared symmetry, both terms drop. Summing a fully-symmetric rank-3 tensor:"},"2832":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-23","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#reductions-unique-inputs--unique-outputs--extra-ops","content":"`mean` adds one divide per unique output cell on top of the sum cost. `median` / `percentile` / `quantile` use a separate selection-style charge — see `flops.tier2_reduction_cost(...)`."},"2833":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-24","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Two public inspection functions return the same `AccumulationCost` shape:"},"2834":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-25","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#inspecting-what-gets-charged","content":"Both are LRU-cached; warm-call latency is in microseconds. See `flops.einsum_cache_info()` and `flops.reduction_cache_info()` for cache state."},"2835":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-26","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"The orbit savings come from the **output** having symmetric structure. Declared symmetry on an input doesn't automatically translate to savings — the question is always whether the *output* cells share orbits."},"2836":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-27","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"**Gotcha 1 — input symmetry that doesn't reach the output.** Declaring `A` symmetric in `'ij,j->i'` doesn't save anything: the output index `i` doesn't share an orbit with anything under the (i, j) swap, so every output cell stays distinct."},"2837":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-28","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"**Gotcha 2 — reduction along an axis that breaks the symmetry.** Summing along axis 0 of a `(6, 6)` `S₂` matrix leaves a `(6,)` output. The `(0, 1)` swap doesn't fix axis 0, so the output has no residual symmetry and no savings appear:"},"2838":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-29","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#when-numbers-might-surprise-you","content":"If your symmetry savings aren't materializing, check whether the operation's *output* would still carry symmetric structure given how the indices flow."},"2839":{"id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection-30","page_id":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/symmetry-detection#whats-actually-happening","content":"For curious contributor-level readers: the model is the α/M direct-event count, where α counts orbits of input entries projected onto the