ssWarning`. Treat the warning system\nas helpful guidance, not as a complete audit of every same-axis subgroup\nchange."],[351,"One-time setup"],[1021,"only unique matrix entries matter"],[1536,"op"],[1659,"Op"],[1714,"Op"],[1759,"Op"],[1770,"Op"],[1812,"Op"],[1857,"Op"],[1952,"Op"],[1994,"Op"],[2027,"Op"],[2176,"Op"],[2249,"Op"],[2276,"Op"],[2323,"Op"],[2348,"Op"],[2411,"Op"],[2434,"Op"],[2460,"Op"],[2513,"Op"],[2540,"Op"],[2573,"Op"],[2590,"Op"],[2618,"Op"],[2078,"Op / family"],[719,"Operation"],[756,"Operation"],[772,"Operation"],[793,"Operation"],[1014,"Operation"],[1059,"Operation"],[2742,"Operation Categories"],[2740,"Operation Categories -- which operations are free, counted, or blocked"],[855,"Operation Categories — what's supported and what isn't"],[121,"Operation Cost Index"],[70,"operation metadata (`op.*`)"],[574,"Operation name (e.g., `\"einsum\"`, `\"exp\"`)"],[2802,"Operations not relevant to numerical computation. Calling them raises an `AttributeError`. These are I/O, configuration, datetime, and display functions that have no meaningful FLOP cost."],[2616,"Operations that apply a user-supplied callable across an array. flopscope bills the\nresult the wrapper materializes (numpy runs the callback itself)."],[2755,"Operations that involve no arithmetic computation — just memory allocation, reshaping, or data movement."],[2757,"Operations that perform arithmetic. Cost is computed analytically from tensor shapes."],[1317,"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a **non-sequential memory access** —\ncomparison-sort order, binary search, a computed-index gather, or a random-reorder draw\n— are billed at weight 4.0. `flop_cost` stays the plain algorithmic count (comparisons,\nindex dereferences, or draws); the 4× comes entirely from the weight, the same\none-factor-does-one-job split the transcendental tier uses."],[1315,"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a libm minimax polynomial\nevaluation (sin, cos, tan, exp, log, arcsin, arccos, arctan, arcsinh,\narccosh, arctanh, power, and their NumPy 2.x aliases) are billed at weight\n16.0.  The `flop_cost` formula is `numel(output)` (1 per element); the 16×\nfactor is supplied entirely by the weight."],[1264,"Ops that compute wider than their inputs"],[1658,"Ops that do more than one accumulation pass carry the extra passes in\n`flop_cost` (never in the weight column): the variance family makes four\npasses (mean-sum, centre, square, variance-sum), `ptp` makes two (max + min)\nplus the per-output subtract, and `mean`/`average` add the per-output divide.\nA `nan*` variant adds a further `numel(input)` on top of its plain sibling's\nformula, for the `isnan` test pass the `nan*` form runs before reducing that\nits plain sibling does not — **but only where NumPy actually runs that pass**.\nNine of the factory-built `nan*` reductions go through NumPy's\n`_replace_nan`, which returns no mask for a non-inexact dtype, so an\n**integer or bool input runs no `isnan` pass and bills exactly like its plain\nsibling**; only float/complex input carries the surcharge. `nanmedian`,\n`nanpercentile`, and `nanquantile` instead go through `_remove_nan_1d`, which\ncalls `np.isnan&#x60; whatever the dtype, so those three carry it for every dtype.\n**`nanmax` and `nanmin` carry it at no dtype at all**: for any plain\nnon-object ndarray — which is every operand flopscope hands them — NumPy\ntakes a fast path that reduces with `fmax`/`fmin` and then tests the *reduced\noutput*, never the input, so there is no input-sized pass to charge and both\nbill exactly like `max`/`min`. Like every other\npass in these ops, the surcharge is **orbit-mapped**: on a symmetric operand it\ncounts unique elements, not dense `numel`. The pass is folded into the same\n`flop_cost` as the rest of the reduction, so it is charged as one additional\npass over the input priced at the op's own rate/factor — not a separate flat\nrate: it inherits the host op's complex factor (e.g. 6.0 on `nanprod`) and\nits resolved accumulator dtype (e.g. the widened int64 rate on an int32\ninput), the same way the variance family and `ptp` already blend multiple\npasses under one op-level factor."],[34,"ops.json"],[1940,"optimal chain matmul cost, each step `matmul_cost(m,k,n)` = `2mkn − mn`; two arrays with a 0-d operand: `numel(other)` (NumPy delegates that form to `dot`, a scalar multiply)"],[1767,"optimal chain order"],[797,"Optimal chain ordering"],[550,"Optimization strategies for reducing FLOP consumption"],[1423,"ordering compare"],[374,"Other types (`chore`, `docs`, …)"],[919,"Out of scope; flopscope models a single budget per program region."],[2500,"output order derived by comparing values — access-tier"],[1486,"Outside the accumulator machinery above, several numpy kernels simply run their\narithmetic at a wider dtype than the input's own — the bill follows where the\ncomputation actually happens, not the input's label:"],[971,"Override `config()` to return the keyword arguments that `__init__` needs to\nreconstruct your model with the right shapes. `from_file` passes them verbatim:"],[399,"Owner: `AIcrowd`"],[1183,"Package"],[1191,"Package"],[1558,"Packing is **not banned and not judged** — it is priced so it does not pay:"],[802,"Pass a `SymmetricTensor` to get automatic cost reductions:"],[264,"Passed"],[317,"Passes in isolation but fails under the parametrize sweep — order-dependent state pollution, not yet root-caused"],[367,"patch"],[366,"patch (`0.2.0` → `0.2.1`)"],[158,"Path"],[608,"Path caching"],[914,"Pattern"],[389,"Pauses for approval in the `pypi` GitHub environment (manual gate).\nA single approval click covers all matrix-publish jobs."],[1692,"per output slice, piecewise in axis length `n` and `q.size` `k` — unweighted: `n·min(k, 1 + 4⌈log₂ min(k,n)⌉) + 4k`; with `weights=`: `4n⌈log₂ n⌉ + 3n + k(⌈log₂ n⌉ + 4)` (+numel(input) for the `nan*` forms, every dtype)"],[882,"Per-instance state?"],[1992,"Per-matrix cost is multiplied by the batch dimension p